   The poll driver matched `Leap status : Normal` on its FIRST read, and the
   `Ref time (UTC) Wed Oct 21 23:11:44` is 4 SECONDS after the configuration was applied
   at 23:11:40 UTC. So this DUT synchronised to an IPv6 server in about 4 s.
   (Contrast, same DUT, same server, IPv4, yesterday: about 75 s. The difference is the
   SIZE of the error to be corrected, not the address family -- yesterday the DUT's clock
   was 14.836 s out; today it was 2.241 s out. It is NOT an IPv6-vs-IPv4 difference and
   must not be reported as one.)

   CORRECTED LATER IN THIS RUN, and the correction is recorded rather than quietly
   rewritten: when step 4 read `show log | include chronyd`, it showed that this step did
   NOT merely agree with a clock that was already right. The DUT measured and removed a
   real 2.241471 s error through the IPv6 association, 3 s after the configuration:
2026 Oct 21 23:11:41 daemon.warning awplus chronyd[22637]: System clock wrong by 2.241471 seconds
2026 Oct 21 23:11:43 daemon.warning awplus chronyd[22637]: System clock was stepped by 2.241471 seconds
   The microsecond-level `Last offset` read above is what was LEFT after that step, not
   the error the DUT started with. So step 2 already contains a real clock correction
   made over IPv6; step 4 then repeats it with an error of a size chosen in advance.

