  MEASURED PLATFORM FACTS worth recording (both are display/encoding behaviour, not
  defects, and both would trip a naive parser):

  (a) FOR AN IPv6 SERVER, `Reference ID` IS A HASH, NOT THE ADDRESS. The DUT prints
      `Reference ID : 09D7146F (fd32:b1f0:dff8:1603::1)`. 09D7146F is NOT any part of
      that address -- it is the 32-bit refid NTPv4 derives for an IPv6 source (RFC 5905
      uses the first 32 bits of the MD5 hash of the address, because a 128-bit address
      cannot fit the 32-bit refid field). For the IPv4 server yesterday the same field
      read `0A261621 (10.38.22.33)`, which IS the address, byte for byte, in hex. So the
      hex half of this field means two completely different things depending on family,
      and only the parenthesised half is the address. Anyone grading an IPv6 NTP case by
      matching the hex against the configured address will read a correct device as wrong.

  (b) `show ntp associations` WRAPS AN IPv6 REMOTE ONTO ITS OWN LINE. The `remote` column
      is sized for an IPv4 literal, so an IPv6 address overflows it and the refid,
      stratum, reach, delay and offset for that peer appear on the FOLLOWING line. The
      `*` system-peer marker stays on the address line. A single-line regex of the form
      `^\* <address>.*reach` therefore matches NOTHING on IPv6, although the device is
      working perfectly. Read the pair of lines, not one line.

  => STEP 2 PASS: the DUT selected the reachable IPv6 server, resolved it on the correct
     leg, reached stratum 6 from a stratum-5 source, and reported itself synchronised with
     a microsecond-level offset and a settled 64.7 s poll. The unreachable IPv6 control
     address was never used: reach 0, no system-peer marker, and a poll interval backing
     off 128 -> 256 s.

