ntpwire: 88 frames in the capture, 16 NTP packets on port(s) 123
  by family: IPv6=16
  by mode:   client(3)=8  server(4)=8
  fd32:b1f0:dff8:1603::1  -> fd32:b1f0:dff8:1603::34   8
  fd32:b1f0:dff8:1603::34 -> fd32:b1f0:dff8:1603::1    8
  pairing:   requests=8 replies=8  paired=8 unpaired=0 unanswered=0

  READING IT, claim by claim:

  1. THE DUT ORIGINATES IPv6 NTP. All 16 NTP packets are `by family: IPv6=16`. There is
     not one IPv4 NTP packet in the capture. The 8 client(3) packets have source
     fd32:b1f0:dff8:1603::34, which is the address this case put on the DUT's vlan10.
     The DUT is the ORIGINATOR, not a responder: mode 3 is the client mode.
  2. IT WAS ANSWERED, AND THE ANSWERS ARE REALLY ITS OWN. `paired=8` means ntpwire matched
     every one of the 8 replies to the request whose transmit timestamp it echoes in the
     origin field. `unpaired=0 unanswered=0`. An unpaired reply would NOT have been counted
     as an answer.
  3. THE CONTROL FLOW DID NOT MATCH -- AND THIS IS THE DECIDING HALF.
     fd32:b1f0:dff8:1603::99 was configured as an `ntp server` at exactly the same moment,
     on the same interface, in the same capture window of 300 s. It does not appear in the
     address-pair summary AT ALL: ZERO packets to it and ZERO from it. The association
     table agrees (`reach 0`, no `*`, poll backing off 128 -> 256 s). So "the DUT polls its
     configured IPv6 server" is not a statement about anything that merely happens to be on
     the wire -- the DUT polled the server that answers and did not use the one that does
     not.
  4. THE POLL SCHEDULE IS VISIBLE AND CORRECT. Four polls about 2 s apart at the start
     (23:11:39, :41, :43, :45 -- the initial sampling burst), then a settled 64 s cadence:
     23:12:49, 23:13:54, 23:14:58, 23:16:03. That matches `Update interval : 64.7 seconds`
     read from the DUT, from an entirely independent instrument.
  5. VERSION AND STRATUM ON THE WIRE. Every DUT request is `v4` (NTPv4) with `st=0`
     (a client does not claim a stratum); every reply is `v4 st=5 refid=10.32.16.2`.
     That is the box's ntpd and its own upstream, and it is where the DUT's `Stratum : 6`
     comes from.
  6. NO KISS-O'-DEATH HERE, and that is worth saying. The queue warned to budget for one,
     because tb277's /etc/ntp.conf carries
     `restrict -6 default kod notrap nomodify nopeer noquery limited` and the IPv4 case's
     COLD BOOT tripped it on 2026-10-21. It was NOT tripped here. The difference is the
     shape of the burst: configuring the client produced 4 polls spaced about 2 s apart,
     whereas a cold boot produces two polls IN THE SAME MILLISECOND from two source ports.
     The rate limiter reacts to the second shape, not the first. (The cold-boot limb is
     step 6 below, where it is expected again.)
  7. CONFIRMED ON IPv6: THE CLIENT RANDOMISES EVERYTHING (platforms/x960.md §7H.5, until
     now measured only on IPv4).
      - Source port, eight polls, eight different ports: 39587, 33177, 54290, 58882,
        59060, 57990, 60260, 57222. A capture filter written for udp SOURCE port 123 would
        have missed every single DUT packet.
      - Request transmit timestamp, which is NOT the DUT's clock and must never be read as
        it: 08:08:15, 17:10:14, 20:38:07, 00:12:53, 21:42:25, 22:10:15, 12:37:54,
        03:09:44 -- scattered across the whole day while the real time was 23:11-23:16.
        The SERVER's transmit timestamps in the replies are the true time and agree with
        the capture timestamps to under a millisecond.

  => STEP 3 PASS: 8 IPv6 client requests from the DUT, 8 server replies, 8 paired, 0
     unanswered, 0 packets of any kind to or from the unreachable control address, in one
     300 s capture on the leg. The DUT is an IPv6 NTP client on the wire.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
STEP 4 -- A REAL CLOCK CORRECTION, MADE THROUGH THE IPv6 ASSOCIATION
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Why this step exists. Steps 2 and 3 prove the DUT polls an IPv6 server and is answered.
They do not by themselves prove it USES the answers to steer its clock -- today the DUT's
clock was already right to within microseconds, so there was nothing to correct and no
correction to observe. The only way to measure a correction is to inject an error of a
size known in advance and watch the DUT remove it.

The CLI form was read read-only first, never `<cmd>` + CR
(memory never-send-cli-help-through-a-cr-driver):
awplus#clock set ?
  HH:MM:SS  Set current time
awplus#clock set 23:00:00 ?
  <1-31>  Day of the month
awplus#clock set 23:00:00 21 ?
  MONTH  Current month (Jan, Feb, ...)
awplus#clock set 23:00:00 21 Oct ?
  <2000-2035>  Current year

The capture was started FIRST:
tb277$ tools/tb capture tb277 ntp61022 eth3 480 t44299-s4-clockfix.pcap udp port 123

The injected error, computed from the BOX's clock at the moment of injection, so its size
is known exactly and is not an estimate:
