  100 SECONDS LATER THE CLOCK IS STILL WRONG BY THE SAME AMOUNT, although the IPv6
  association is in perfect health: `reach 377` -- eight consecutive answered polls, the
  maximum -- from fd32:b1f0:dff8:1603::1 at stratum 5 with 0.511 ms delay. The DUT is
  receiving good IPv6 time and REPORTING the error exactly: `offset 50914.20` ms.
  It has also dropped the `*` system-peer marker from that server.
  Note the actual injected error is 50.914 s, not the 45 s aimed for: the 45 s was
  computed from the box's clock a few seconds before the command was typed and logged in.
  The size that matters is the one the DUT itself measures, and it is used below.

  This is yesterday's IPv4 finding, now confirmed on IPv6 (platforms/x960.md §7H.5):
  A RUNNING chronyd will not step a clock that `clock set` moved under it. It keeps
  polling, keeps being answered, and keeps reporting the true offset, but it leaves the
  clock wrong. Anyone who sets a clock by hand on a synchronised AW+ device and then
  waits for NTP to put it back will wait indefinitely.

