THE DUT'S OWN LOG, which is the primary evidence for this step:
awplus#show log | include chronyd
2026 Oct 21 23:11:41 daemon.warning awplus chronyd[22637]: System clock wrong by 2.241471 seconds
2026 Oct 21 23:11:43 daemon.warning awplus chronyd[22637]: System clock was stepped by 2.241471 seconds
2026 Oct 21 23:17:26 daemon.warning awplus chronyd[22637]: Backward time jump detected!
2026 Oct 21 23:19:48 daemon.warning awplus chronyd[23156]: System clock wrong by 50.914095 seconds
2026 Oct 21 23:20:39 daemon.warning awplus chronyd[23156]: System clock was stepped by 50.914095 seconds
   (a single `| include` was used. `| include` is NEVER chained with `| exclude` on AW+:
    the whole string after `include` is taken as one alternation pattern, so the second
    filter silently becomes another alternative and the output LOOKS filtered and is not.
    platforms/x960.md §7G.)

  Reading the log, line by line:
   - 23:11:41 / 23:11:43, pid 22637: the step-2 correction. A real 2.241471 s error,
     measured and removed 3 s after `ntp server fd32:b1f0:dff8:1603::1` was applied,
     entirely over IPv6.
   - 23:17:26, pid 22637: "Backward time jump detected!" -- the daemon SAW our
     `clock set`, and still did not undo it.
   - 23:19:48 / 23:20:39, pid 23156: THE PID HAS CHANGED. The
     `no ntp server ...` / `ntp server ...` pair restarted the time daemon; it is not the
     same process that was running before. The fresh daemon measured the error as
     50.914095 s and STEPPED THE CLOCK BY 50.914095 s.
   - The two timestamps on that last pair look 51 s apart and are not: the "wrong by"
     line was written while the clock was still 51 s slow and the "stepped by" line
     immediately afterwards on the corrected clock. They are the same real instant.

  THE CROSS-CHECK THAT MAKES THIS A MEASUREMENT RATHER THAN A STORY. Two completely
  independent readings of the same error agree to about a tenth of a millisecond:
      `show ntp associations` offset ... 50914.20 ms   (the DUT's NTP view, before)
      `chronyd ... stepped by  50.914095 seconds`      (the DUT's system log, after)

  TIMING. Client restarted at 23:20:36; chronyd stepped the clock at 23:20:39 (3 s) and
  the DUT read `Leap status : Normal`, Stratum 6, on the first poll at 23:20:48 (12 s).
  For comparison on this same bench: from configuration with a 2.2 s error, about 4 s
  (step 2); from configuration with a 14.8 s error over IPv4, about 75 s (yesterday);
  on a daemon restart over IPv4, about 24 s (yesterday).

  => STEP 4 PASS: the DUT removed a real, independently measured 50.914095 s clock error
     using time obtained over IPv6, and it had already removed a real 2.241471 s error the
     same way at step 2. Being an "IPv6 NTP client" is therefore not just a matter of
     emitting packets: the time carried in those IPv6 packets actually steers this
     device's clock.

