STEP 7 -- TIME TO RE-ACQUIRE FROM COLD, AND THE KISS-O'-DEATH THAT DID NOT HAPPEN
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
    time (UTC)   event                                            elapsed from login
    ----------   -----                                            ------------------
    23:31:18     login prompt                                     0
    23:31:30     leg passes traffic 4/4                           12 s
    23:31:30     `chronyd[2289]: System clock was stepped
                  by 0.655756 seconds`                            12 s
    23:31:38     `Leap status : Normal`, Stratum 6,
                 `* fd32:b1f0:dff8:1603::1 ... reach 7`           20 s

  RE-ACQUISITION FROM COLD: about 12 s to the clock step, about 20 s to a confirmed
  synchronised read. The poll driver was armed to wait 660 s and MATCHED on its FIRST
  read, so no part of this number is a timeout.

🔴 THE KISS-O'-DEATH BUDGETED FOR DID NOT OCCUR, AND THAT IS ITSELF THE MEASUREMENT.
  The dispatch and the campaign queue both warned to expect one: tb277's /etc/ntp.conf
  carries `restrict -6 default kod notrap nomodify nopeer noquery limited`, and on
  2026-10-21 the IPv4 client's COLD BOOT tripped it, drew a stratum-0 reply with
  refid `RATE`, and cost 10 MINUTES 55 SECONDS to converge. Today's IPv6 cold boot drew
  no kiss-o'-death at all and converged in 20 s -- 33 times faster.

  This is reported as a DIFFERENCE OBSERVED, not as an explanation. What would have to be
  checked before anyone claims a cause:
   - the `restrict -6` line is the IPv6 restriction, so it was armed for this leg;
   - the trigger yesterday was the SHAPE of the boot burst (two polls in the same
     millisecond from two source ports), not the protocol;
   - this run's boot burst is in t44299-s6-persist.pcap and is decoded below.
  What must NOT be written anywhere is "IPv6 NTP recovers faster than IPv4 NTP". Nothing
  in this case measures that. A slow convergence on this bench is a property of the
  BENCH'S OWN ntpd rate limiter, and a fast one is the absence of that limiter firing.

  => STEP 7 PASS: re-acquisition from a cold boot was measured, not assumed: 20 s from the
     login prompt to a confirmed `Leap status : Normal` at Stratum 6, with a real 0.655756 s
     clock step on the way. The 0.655756 s is also a measurement of this DUT's real-time
     clock drift across a 63 s reboot -- it keeps time across the power interruption and
     loses about two thirds of a second doing it. (Yesterday, across a 60 s reboot:
     0.592688 s. The same order, from the same hardware.)

